{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The team that spent a sprint debugging their own retry logic</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A small team built an agent that triages support tickets: read the ticket, call a few internal APIs, draft a reply. It started as a simple loop — call the model, run whatever tool it asked for, feed the result back — and it worked. Six months later, that same team spent an entire sprint debugging why some tickets got processed twice after a deploy, why a slow API call sometimes made the whole agent silently hang, and why nobody could tell which step a stuck ticket was actually stuck on.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of that sprint touched the agent's actual logic — the part that decides what a good triage reply looks like. All of it was infrastructure the team had hand-rolled around the loop: a retry mechanism that occasionally double-fired, a persistence layer that lost in-flight state on redeploy, and no real visibility into which step of which ticket's run was currently executing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team's core agent logic — prompt, tools, triage rules — had been stable for months. Nearly all of their recent engineering time on this feature had gone into orchestration concerns that have nothing to do with triage specifically: at-least-once vs. exactly-once tool execution, surviving a mid-run deploy, and giving a human a way to see where a given run currently stood. Those are the same problems every team running an agent in production eventually hits, solved from scratch, again, by a team whose actual job was ticket triage.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the generic shape of "premature infrastructure debt" applied to agents specifically: the loop itself is trivial to write, which makes it easy to underestimate everything durable execution actually requires — idempotent step tracking, crash-safe state persistence, backoff-with-jitter retries, and structured tracing — until a production incident forces the team to build all of it anyway, under worse time pressure and with less design care than a dedicated platform already applied to the same problem.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Graduate when the infra, not the agent, is the bottleneck</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A hand-rolled agent loop is the right starting point — it's fast to build and easy to understand end to end. A hosted agent platform (the kind of managed runtime that runs your agent loop for you) earns its cost once the team is spending more time on things like "what happens if this step fails halfway through," "how do we see what our agent is currently doing," and "how do we make sure a retry doesn't run a side effect twice" than on the actual decision-making logic of the agent itself. Those are exactly the problems a hosted platform is built to solve once, well, for every agent that runs on it — not problems specific to any one team's use case.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Concretely: persistence (a run's state survives a redeploy or a crash without losing progress or restarting from scratch), retries (a failed step retries with backoff, and a successful step isn't accidentally re-executed with a side effect the second time), observability (a human can see exactly which step of which run is executing right now, and what it did, without grepping logs across services), and scaling (many concurrent runs don't require the team to hand-manage worker pools or queue depth) are the four recurring needs. A team hitting real production pain on two or more of these, rather than hypothesizing about them in advance, is the actual signal — not a fixed timeline or headcount threshold.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The graduation decision is really a build-vs-buy call scoped narrowly to orchestration, not to the agent's domain logic — moving to a hosted platform doesn't change what the agent decides or how it's prompted, it changes who's responsible for durable execution semantics underneath it. That's a meaningfully smaller migration than it sounds: the agent's actual decision logic (prompts, tool definitions, business rules) is usually portable, while the loop, retry, and persistence code is what gets replaced — which is also why waiting too long to graduate isn't fatal, just increasingly expensive, since it's the surrounding scaffolding that has to be unwound, not the core logic rewritten.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The team that graduated a quarter too early — and the one that waited too long</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      One team adopted a hosted agent platform for a single internal tool used by three people, before they'd hit any real operational pain — they spent two weeks learning the platform's concepts and adapting their simple loop to its execution model, for a workload that had never actually failed in a way persistence or retries would have fixed. The migration cost more than the problem it solved.
    </p>
    <p>
      A different team kept hand-rolling their retry and persistence logic long after hitting duplicate side effects in production twice, reasoning each incident was "a one-off, we patched it." By the third incident — a customer-facing action executed twice because a redeploy interrupted an in-flight retry — the fix finally was migrating to a hosted platform, at a point where the surrounding hand-rolled infrastructure had grown large and tangled enough that separating it from the actual agent logic took real, dedicated migration work.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Both mistakes are versions of the same error: not tying the decision to an observed signal. The early team graduated on a hypothetical ("this might need to scale someday"); the late team kept patching symptoms individually instead of recognizing that three separate incidents against the same four categories — persistence, retries, observability, scaling — were the same underlying signal appearing three times, not three unrelated bugs.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The actionable version of "watch for the signal" is auditing engineering time allocation on the feature every so often, not just counting incidents — if a team's recent commits and recent incident write-ups on an agent feature are dominated by orchestration concerns (retry logic, persistence bugs, "why did this run twice") rather than by changes to what the agent actually does, that ratio itself is the graduation signal, whether or not it's yet produced a customer-visible incident.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

