{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Graduating doesn't mean giving up control</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Moving to a hosted platform doesn't mean losing the ability to customize how the agent behaves — the prompt, the tool definitions, and the business logic for what a good response looks like still live in code the team owns and edits directly. What moves to the platform is specifically the execution scaffolding underneath: how a step's state gets saved, how a retry gets scheduled, how a run's progress gets surfaced.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A useful gut check before graduating: list every incident or debugging session from the last quarter related to this agent feature, and tag each one "agent logic" (the prompt made a bad decision, a tool call had wrong parameters) or "orchestration" (a retry misfired, state was lost, a run appeared stuck with no visibility). If orchestration tags dominate, that's the concrete evidence for the graduation call — not a vague sense that "this feels like it needs to be more robust."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Watch for the reverse anti-pattern too: treating "we're on a hosted platform now" as license to stop thinking about failure modes at the agent-logic layer. A platform gives you durable execution of whatever steps you defined — it won't notice that a tool call's retry is semantically unsafe to repeat (a payment charge, an email send) unless you've explicitly designed that step to be idempotent or gated the platform's retry behavior around it. Graduating solves the orchestration-layer problems; it doesn't retroactively make every step in your agent safe to retry.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

